{"kind":"request","text":"Establish a reproducible performance evidence lane for Gitseq. The ratified fast-status design at #632 recorded useful point measurements and explicit latency, size, and bounded-work goals, and later work delivered checkpoints, resident snapshots, bounded summaries, and focused benchmarks. Those results never became a maintained baseline: the repository has no versioned workload contract, no retained comparison history, and no dedicated performance lane. Define deterministic generated fixtures and complete-operation scenarios that cover log depth, checkpoint-tail boundaries, payload size, projection shape, concurrency, cold and warm lifecycle paths, failure fallbacks, and a bounded soak. Measure latency distributions, throughput, CPU, allocations, peak and steady memory, garbage collection, Git child-process count and cumulative duration, filesystem work where available, queue or shared-flight wait, checkpoint cost, response construction and encoding, and an equivalence digest against the trusted result. Record the generator version and seed plus Go, Git, OS, CPU, filesystem, and container limits with every result. Compare candidate and main with repeated samples and benchstat, retaining raw output and profiles on regression. Provide an opt-in local command and a stable manual or scheduled runner outside the every-change correctness review. Re-evaluate the old 500 ms, 2 s, 32 KiB, and 64 KiB goals as hypotheses and publish the first current baseline rather than silently treating them as present guarantees.","body":{"branch":"request/performance-evidence-lane","conditions":"A versioned deterministic fixture and scenario contract covers at least depths 100, 1000, 10000, and 100000 plus checkpoint tails 0, 1, 10, 255, 256, and 1000; complete scenarios include startup, cold and warm status, submit to durable acknowledgement, submit to waiter notification, checkpoint restart, honest fallback, quiet long-poll, concurrent read and write, and a bounded soak; results retain environment metadata, raw samples, p50/p95/p99/max where applicable, CPU, allocation and memory evidence, Git process work, checkpoint and encoding costs, and a correctness digest; candidate versus main uses repeated samples and benchstat; setup cost is reported separately from measured operation cost; the lane is opt-in or scheduled and is not run by the ordinary make test review gate; the first baseline states which #632 goals are met, missed, or not yet measurable; implementation is on the named request branch and receives independent exact-head review before merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}